  "unit": "latin",
  "title": "Throw and Send: ject, mit and miss",
  "short": "ject means throw; mit and miss mean send or let go.",
  "trouble": "Learners miss the root when the prefix changes it: submit (sub + mit), demise (de + mit).",
  "prereqs": [
    "roots.ced-gress"
  ],
  "xp": 1,
  "viz": [
    "vo-roots",
    "vo-word"
  ],
  "figureArchetype": "GeneralFigure",
  "lesson": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "To <i>reject</i> is to throw back, to <i>project</i> is to throw forward and to <i>eject</i> is to throw out. <b>ject</b> means “throw.” A second root, <b>mit</b> (also <b>miss</b>), means “send” or “let go”: to <i>transmit</i> is to send across, a <i>mission</i> is a sending and to <i>dismiss</i> is to send away."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-scene",
        "opts": {
